<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gyeongbokgung</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1395">1395</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Gyeongbok Palace</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gyeongbokgung is the main royal palace of the Joseon dynasty in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seoul">Seoul</a>, built in 1395 after the capital moved to Hanyang. It was burned during the Japanese invasion of 1592 and left in ruins for more than two centuries until the regent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heungseon-daewongun">Heungseon Daewongun</a> rebuilt it in 1867. Queen Min was murdered there in 1895. Much of the palace was demolished under Japanese rule, and it has been extensively restored since the 1990s.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>After founding Joseon in 1392, King Taejo decided in 1394 that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seoul">Hanyang</a>, now Seoul, would be the capital. The first phase of the palace was completed in 1395, and the official Chŏng Tojŏn named it Gyeongbokgung, meaning great blessings palace, after the last characters of a poem in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/classic-of-poetry">Classic of Poetry</a>. Its site weighed access to water, farmland and roads, and followed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rites-of-zhou">Rites of Zhou</a>, which put the ancestral temple on the left and the soil and grain temple on the right. Feng shui placed a mountain behind it and water in front.</p><p>The layout is mostly symmetrical along a north–south axis, with three gates and three courtyards: the first for government offices, the second for the king's dealings with his subjects, and the third where the king's family lived. Chŏng named the main buildings from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-documents">Book of Documents</a> to express Neo-Confucian principles, and his arguments for the site were preferred to those of the Buddhist monk Muhak.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>In 1398 the capital moved to Kaegyŏng and the palace stood abandoned for about ten years. King Taejong, who returned in 1405, preferred Changdeokgung. In 1421 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sejong-the-great">Sejong the Great</a> made Gyeongbokgung his primary palace and expanded it. It then held a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/water-clock">water clock</a>, a facility for producing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/movable-type">movable type</a>, the observatory Ganuidae, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hall-of-worthies">Hall of Worthies</a>, which helped him develop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hangul">Hangul</a>. In 1592, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imjin-war">Imjin War</a>, the palace was completely burned. Who set the fire is debated, since it was intact when the Japanese entered the city. For about 270 years it went mostly unused, because money was short and other palaces sufficed.</p><p>The regent for King Gojong ordered reconstruction, which was finally completed in 1888, after fires in 1873 and 1876. In 1887 the first electric light in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> was lit there. After Queen Min was assassinated in 1895, Gojong did not live there again.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>In 1907 the palace became a public park, and in 1910 its property was auctioned off. Of around 500 buildings in 1888, only 40 remained by the end of the colonial period. The Government-General of Chōsen Building, built in the palace between 1916 and 1926, was demolished in 1995–1996. The First Restoration Plan, begun in 1990, restored 89 buildings and was completed in 2010, when about 25% of the original buildings stood. A second plan, running to 2045, will recreate 90 more. The authenticity of recreated buildings remains debated, partly because Joseon builders left few records. The palace now houses the National Palace Museum of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> and the National Folk Museum of Korea.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gyeongbokgung” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1394 the capital moved from Kaegyong to Hanyang, renamed Hanseong and today Seoul, where <b>Gyeongbokgung</b> palace and city walls were built.</blockquote><a href="/empire/joseon">Joseon · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The colonial authorities destroyed 85 percent of all the buildings in <b>Gyeongbokgung</b> Palace, and built the Japanese General Government Building on the exact site of its primary building.</blockquote><a href="/empire/korea-under-japanese-rule">Korea under Japanese rule · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>One account holds that the song spread to Seoul and the rest of Korea when workers from Gangwon were sent to rebuild <b>Gyeongbok Palace</b> under the Prince Regent Heungseon; some trace the words of its refrain to the cries of their families as the officials took them away.</blockquote><a href="/songs/arirang">Arirang</a></li></ul></section>
